Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script which removes comments from Vim script files using tree-sitter,
with the following requirements: The script should be an executable Python 3
tool (Termux-compatible shebang) that processes one or more Vim script files in
parallel using multiprocessing.
It uses the tree-sitter library with the tree-sitter-vim grammar package (loaded
via its language function, assigned to a Parser's language attribute) to parse
each file's bytes, then walks the syntax tree recursively, collecting byte
ranges of every node whose type is "comment" without descending into those
nodes.
If no comment ranges exist, the original content is returned unchanged with a
count of zero; otherwise the content is rebuilt by concatenating the non-comment
byte segments in order, tracking the end offset of the last removed comment, and
returning the new bytes along with the number of comments removed.
The agent should implement a dataclass for per-file results holding the path, a
success flag, the number of comments removed, an error message string (empty by
default), and the processing time in seconds.
A remover class should construct the parser once in its initializer and expose a
method that takes raw bytes and returns cleaned bytes plus a comment count, with
private helpers for the node-type check and the recursive range collection.
The main flow should accept file paths from the command line arguments, validate
them, and process each file in a worker pool created with the multiprocessing
module, with each worker creating its own remover instance.
For each file the worker should read the file as bytes, remove comments, write
the result back to the same file only when comments were removed (or otherwise
handle the write deliberately), measure elapsed time with a monotonic-style
timer, and catch exceptions so that failures are reported in the result object's
error field rather than crashing the pool.
Each result should be reported with the file name, success status, number of
comments removed, and timing, and the script should print a summary of total
files, successes, failures, and total comments removed.
The script should exit with a nonzero status if any file failed, and print a
usage message and exit with an error when no arguments are provided.
Include edge-case handling: nonexistent paths or directories should be reported
as errors without aborting the batch, empty files should be handled gracefully,
files with no comments should not be rewritten, and non-UTF-8 or binary input
should be processed as raw bytes without decoding.
Use type hints throughout, including support for string or Path path-like inputs
via a type alias and postponed annotation evaluation.
The output must be the prompt only. ---
